WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:09.923
Femi Oke: I have a theory
that Africans have this hotline to joy.

00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:11.550
Angélique Kidjo: Oh yeah.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.760
FO: We greet each other,
we welcome each other.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.304
There's, within us as a continent,

00:00:16.304 --> 00:00:18.181
there is great joy.

00:00:18.223 --> 00:00:19.391
AK: Absolutely.

00:00:19.391 --> 00:00:21.142
It's a state of mind for us.

00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:23.895
It's either laugh or die.

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:25.522
We don't have the choice.

00:00:25.522 --> 00:00:28.942
And that's where the proverbs come from,

00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:32.696
that's where all those mimic, sometime --

00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:34.030
can be an insult,

00:00:34.072 --> 00:00:35.824
or going, "Get out of here.

00:00:35.824 --> 00:00:38.159
You're making a fool of yourself."

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.370
Our body, we have signs.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:42.163
And we look at each other

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:43.373
and the situation ...

00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:45.000
it might be a dire situation,

00:00:45.041 --> 00:00:46.710
and then we start laughing and go,

00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:49.671
"Somebody make a huge mistake.

00:00:49.713 --> 00:00:51.131
And you are in a ditch,

00:00:51.172 --> 00:00:53.174
You can't even get yourself out."

00:00:53.216 --> 00:00:55.677
And we laugh and we're happy
at the same time.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:57.721
That's why I say it's a state of mind.

00:00:58.305 --> 00:01:02.142
FO: I have seen people watch you perform,
and they’re in the audience,

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.145
they are mesmerized, they are smiling,

00:01:05.186 --> 00:01:06.813
they are joyful.

00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:11.651
Do you have an awareness
of what you are doing to your audience?

00:01:12.402 --> 00:01:14.029
AK: No, I don't have any awareness

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.532
because for me,
every concert is a start over.

00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:19.909
I never take any public for granted

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:23.246
because you never know
what the concert is going to be.

00:01:23.288 --> 00:01:25.206
You can have plan in your head,

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:27.834
you can have your set list and everything.

00:01:27.876 --> 00:01:30.754
It’s just to help you, it’s just like,

00:01:30.795 --> 00:01:34.174
a parameter that you put in there
to be able to perform.

00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:37.886
But what happens after that
is out of your hands.

00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:39.596
That's the power of music.

00:01:39.638 --> 00:01:42.432
When I'm holding my microphone,

00:01:42.474 --> 00:01:44.476
for me, stage is my sanctuary.

00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:49.397
FO: I was looking up some
of the comments people write

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:52.108
when they watched you online on YouTube.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.569
Now, normally I wouldn't do this,
but this is fine.

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:55.945
This is not scary.

00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:58.281
"Pure love for Mama Angelique.

00:01:58.281 --> 00:02:01.034
A beautiful ball of energy."

00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:03.995
AK: (Laughs)

00:02:04.037 --> 00:02:06.206
FO: You know what you're doing.

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:11.711
AK: Well, being on stage
for me is the most important thing.

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:14.255
I hate to be in studio,

00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:17.008
but I've had to learn throughout the years

00:02:17.008 --> 00:02:20.220
that I need to be in the studio
to be able to perform,

00:02:20.261 --> 00:02:21.429
to be on stage.

00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:26.768
And that energy that we're talking about,
the public give it to me.

00:02:26.768 --> 00:02:28.103
I can't keep it.

00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:30.146
If I keep it, I will explode.

00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:31.564
I mean, just face it.

00:02:31.564 --> 00:02:34.275
And I can't sleep,
like, when I finish a concert,

00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:37.570
it takes me like four hours
for me to dwindle down

00:02:37.612 --> 00:02:38.863
and to be able to sleep.

00:02:38.863 --> 00:02:41.574
It’s terrible, because I’m wired up.

00:02:41.574 --> 00:02:43.827
I don't need no drugs,
I don't drink coffee.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.788
I don't drink alcohol,
I don't take anything, I don't need it.

00:02:46.788 --> 00:02:48.248
The music is my drug.

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:52.252
So therefore, when I'm onstage,

00:02:52.293 --> 00:02:56.506
I'm just trying to convey the happiness,

00:02:56.506 --> 00:02:58.341
the joy

00:02:58.383 --> 00:03:01.678
and the strength
that we have as human beings

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.930
to prevail in every circumstance.

00:03:04.431 --> 00:03:08.017
Because music has shown me,

00:03:08.059 --> 00:03:10.103
when I'm facing the public,

00:03:10.145 --> 00:03:12.564
I see people from around the world,

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:14.858
all different skin tones

00:03:14.899 --> 00:03:18.319
and probably all people
that speak different languages,

00:03:18.361 --> 00:03:20.238
that makes you humble.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.368
FO: And you've gone so beyond
what would be expected of somebody

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.454
with your profile as a musician.

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:30.373
You are not just a musician,
you're not just an artist.

00:03:30.415 --> 00:03:32.250
You are also an educator.

00:03:33.376 --> 00:03:35.086
AK: Education is everything.

00:03:36.045 --> 00:03:41.384
And when I started this journey
with my foundation Batonga,

00:03:41.426 --> 00:03:44.512
one thing I have in mind
is what my father always used to say.

00:03:45.013 --> 00:03:48.224
An educated person is a peacemaker.

00:03:50.018 --> 00:03:51.436
And it's true,

00:03:51.478 --> 00:03:52.896
because when you are educated

00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:56.232
and understand the connectivity,

00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:59.402
everything in the world we live in
is connected, even us.

00:03:59.944 --> 00:04:03.156
And if there's one thing
we learned during the pandemic,

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:05.033
it's that we cannot live alone.

00:04:05.617 --> 00:04:08.661
Lockdown or not, it becomes a point
where we need others

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:11.831
to be able to celebrate our own humanity.

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:16.211
From the moment I started going to school,
it triggered something in me.

00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:19.714
Not only the curiosity,
but I was thirsty of learning.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.091
What can I learn every day?

00:04:22.091 --> 00:04:24.177
And I would come home and I ask questions.

00:04:24.219 --> 00:04:28.223
And I'm lucky enough
to have 10 brothers and sisters

00:04:28.223 --> 00:04:29.432
that come before.

00:04:29.432 --> 00:04:31.017
Some of them have been there.

00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:33.978
So if I ask questions,
somebody's always going to answer me.

00:04:34.646 --> 00:04:38.441
And then I get to high school

00:04:38.483 --> 00:04:41.361
and I start seeing
a different side of education.

00:04:42.153 --> 00:04:44.697
Because when I finished primary school,

00:04:44.739 --> 00:04:47.408
most of my girlfriends, we made plan.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.286
When we get to high school,
we're going to do this and that.

00:04:50.286 --> 00:04:52.580
Some of them disappeared from the radar.

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:57.085
And I’d be asking the question:
“Why aren’t they here?”

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.880
Then, my girlfriend in the same
street where I grew up,

00:05:00.922 --> 00:05:04.843
right across my house,

00:05:04.843 --> 00:05:06.052
never make it to high --

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:08.638
And I'm like, "Dad, what is going on?"

00:05:08.638 --> 00:05:11.349
And then he went and found out
that she got married.

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:12.517
And I was --

00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:14.561
It was a day that I'm like,

00:05:14.602 --> 00:05:17.981
"Is it worth living, if at that age
you're going to marry somebody

00:05:18.022 --> 00:05:20.149
without me knowing what was going on?"

00:05:20.692 --> 00:05:23.611
So to start my foundation,
secondary school,

00:05:23.611 --> 00:05:27.699
is to allow those young girls to buy time.

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:32.245
To have a future, to decide
the future they want to have

00:05:32.287 --> 00:05:34.330
instead of being married young

00:05:34.372 --> 00:05:38.126
and end up dying on a delivering bed.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.130
So for me, education not only

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:46.301
allowed us to understand
the complexity of this world,

00:05:46.301 --> 00:05:49.178
it also unleashed the power

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:54.142
that we have to understand
when we stand in front of injustice,

00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:55.560
our rights,

00:05:55.602 --> 00:05:57.395
what is right and what is wrong.

00:05:58.271 --> 00:05:59.981
And when it comes to women,

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:02.817
especially in the continent of Africa,

00:06:02.817 --> 00:06:08.948
I start realizing that the vision
of my father for women

00:06:08.948 --> 00:06:13.328
is totally different from the vision
of the father of my friends.

00:06:13.369 --> 00:06:15.538
Because when I go to their home,

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.665
I couldn't speak
because it was not allowed.

00:06:17.707 --> 00:06:19.667
You have to be given permission to speak.

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:22.086
While in my house,
you have something in your mind,

00:06:22.128 --> 00:06:23.630
go right ahead.

00:06:23.671 --> 00:06:25.590
Somebody's going to answer you.

00:06:25.965 --> 00:06:28.176
And I start seeing
the differences, and I said,

00:06:28.176 --> 00:06:31.888
“How can we break this cycle

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:36.100
of always women have to be
the ones that get quiet

00:06:36.142 --> 00:06:38.686
and men speak up
even when they say nonsense.

00:06:40.647 --> 00:06:45.109
FO: The Batonga Foundation, it creates
opportunities for women and girls.

00:06:45.109 --> 00:06:47.195
It creates a future for them.

00:06:47.236 --> 00:06:49.447
It sounds easy, though, to say that:

00:06:49.489 --> 00:06:51.991
creates opportunities
for women and girls, easy.

00:06:51.991 --> 00:06:53.326
But it's not.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:59.207
Can you give us an example
of your advocacy work

00:06:59.248 --> 00:07:02.418
that has been difficult

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.212
but you've worked through it

00:07:04.253 --> 00:07:08.299
and you've seen success
and happiness the other side?

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:10.718
AK: The hardest thing ...

00:07:12.553 --> 00:07:17.684
when I started was not being able
to get the girl to speak.

00:07:19.644 --> 00:07:21.354
It was breaking my heart.

00:07:21.688 --> 00:07:22.855
"What is your name?"

00:07:22.897 --> 00:07:24.857
There's no answer, they look at you like.

00:07:26.192 --> 00:07:27.860
And I’m like, “Why?

00:07:29.112 --> 00:07:31.447
Why? Tell me what you need.

00:07:31.489 --> 00:07:33.032
I'm here to help.

00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:35.368
I'm not going to tell you what to do."

00:07:36.786 --> 00:07:41.958
From the day they realize

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.836
and have confidence

00:07:44.836 --> 00:07:47.296
that I'm not here to judge their choices,

00:07:47.338 --> 00:07:49.340
some of them were already mothers.

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:54.262
And you have a lot of pride

00:07:54.303 --> 00:07:57.515
when you're talking
to underprivileged people.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:00.977
Because I decided from the get-go

00:08:00.977 --> 00:08:04.605
to walk the dirt road
where no one wants to go

00:08:04.605 --> 00:08:07.191
because that's where you
have fewer opportunities.

00:08:07.525 --> 00:08:13.406
And I was not going to let those girls
be marginalized and left in limbo.

00:08:14.532 --> 00:08:17.577
So from the moment they start speaking,

00:08:17.618 --> 00:08:19.579
to me,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.207
is the moment I start seeing
how we can move forward together.

00:08:24.333 --> 00:08:28.004
So we start doing,

00:08:28.004 --> 00:08:31.758
as an organization,
we needed data, right?

00:08:32.508 --> 00:08:34.260
Because I've done it ten years,

00:08:34.302 --> 00:08:37.889
sending people to high school,
they end up in university,

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:39.599
all that kind of stuff.

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:42.643
But I wanted to understand why

00:08:43.436 --> 00:08:45.897
and the reason
of the drop out of the girls.

00:08:46.689 --> 00:08:49.233
So through that, we find out
many different reasons.

00:08:49.776 --> 00:08:52.737
One of the reasons of the drop out
is that in primary school,

00:08:52.737 --> 00:08:54.697
the teachers were not good enough,

00:08:54.739 --> 00:08:57.784
so they didn’t have the tools
to succeed in secondary school.

00:08:58.284 --> 00:09:00.578
And there's so much ammunition
you can go through

00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:03.456
when you appear to be a stupid person

00:09:03.456 --> 00:09:05.958
and you're not stupid,
you just don't have the tools.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.502
Then you drop out.

00:09:07.543 --> 00:09:10.254
Pregnancy, of course, and family poverty.

00:09:11.547 --> 00:09:14.550
And one thing that came out
of that data collection

00:09:14.592 --> 00:09:18.846
is all of the girls say,
“We need a safe space.

00:09:19.597 --> 00:09:22.475
We need a place where
we can talk to one another

00:09:22.517 --> 00:09:25.686
and we can work together
and support one another."

00:09:25.978 --> 00:09:27.396
I said, "It's done."

00:09:27.772 --> 00:09:30.108
So we start creating the girl club

00:09:30.149 --> 00:09:34.112
and built a curriculum
based on their need.

00:09:34.946 --> 00:09:39.367
And they ask to be taught
how to prevent rape.

00:09:39.867 --> 00:09:41.536
How can they run away?

00:09:41.994 --> 00:09:43.162
How can they do things?

00:09:43.204 --> 00:09:46.916
So we come up with simple thing.

00:09:47.834 --> 00:09:49.836
Don't go out when it's getting dark.

00:09:50.211 --> 00:09:53.756
When you're walking
and you have a shadow behind you

00:09:53.798 --> 00:09:55.716
and you feel uncomfortable, don't start,

00:09:55.716 --> 00:09:57.218
don't try to be courageous.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.928
Don't try to be daring.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:00.221
Run away.

00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:01.722
And we prepared the village.

00:10:01.764 --> 00:10:04.600
If a girl comes to your house,

00:10:04.642 --> 00:10:07.103
keep her there until
the family member can come

00:10:07.103 --> 00:10:08.980
because there's a danger in the street.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.815
So we start the whole thing like that.

00:10:10.857 --> 00:10:13.526
What is going on today

00:10:13.568 --> 00:10:15.736
that I’m really proud of

00:10:15.778 --> 00:10:19.115
is that those young girls

00:10:19.157 --> 00:10:22.618
are becoming entrepreneurs
and creating jobs.

00:10:23.953 --> 00:10:27.707
They are in total control
of what they do with their money.

00:10:28.457 --> 00:10:30.960
They decided they're going to save money.

00:10:31.002 --> 00:10:32.253
It's not me.

00:10:32.295 --> 00:10:34.547
For the children
not to go through this again.

00:10:35.298 --> 00:10:38.009
What they want is to break
the cycle of poverty.

00:10:38.467 --> 00:10:40.344
The next step that I'm taking

00:10:40.386 --> 00:10:43.181
is to go to the northern part
of my country, Benin.

00:10:44.807 --> 00:10:47.685
The girls in Benin, at eight years old,

00:10:47.685 --> 00:10:51.105
are sent to the man
that's going to marry them home,

00:10:51.105 --> 00:10:53.524
they become maids,

00:10:53.566 --> 00:10:55.193
until they have --

00:10:55.193 --> 00:10:56.777
And I want to break that cycle.

00:10:56.777 --> 00:10:58.112
I've been working hard.

00:10:58.154 --> 00:11:00.489
I’ve been asking for help
with this for so long.

00:11:02.074 --> 00:11:05.494
We're going to start there
with local organizations.

00:11:05.494 --> 00:11:07.914
We don't bring anybody from outside.

00:11:07.955 --> 00:11:10.750
We work with the people
that know the needs.

00:11:10.791 --> 00:11:12.001
We work with the village,

00:11:12.001 --> 00:11:15.087
the head of the villages, the mothers.

00:11:15.129 --> 00:11:19.050
We want people to see for themselves
the progress we are making

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.719
and for them, to allow them
to listen to the kids

00:11:21.761 --> 00:11:23.429
that they never do.

00:11:24.222 --> 00:11:25.473
FO: What is the difference

00:11:25.473 --> 00:11:31.896
between the pleasure you get
from changing a generation's life

00:11:31.938 --> 00:11:38.194
to the pleasure of being
on stage at a major event, performing,

00:11:38.236 --> 00:11:40.613
and the crowd is going wild?

00:11:40.613 --> 00:11:43.282
What's the difference
between that kind of joy?

00:11:44.492 --> 00:11:46.827
AK: They are two totally different joys.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:53.918
The joy of seeing these girls

00:11:53.918 --> 00:11:56.837
being articulate today,

00:11:56.879 --> 00:11:59.590
talking truth to power
to the head of villages

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:02.260
telling them, "No one
is touching us anymore.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.054
No one have the right
to touch my boob, no."

00:12:05.429 --> 00:12:07.223
And they're adamant.

00:12:07.807 --> 00:12:12.687
I mean, the joy at taking that
is that finally,

00:12:12.687 --> 00:12:15.398
you can speak up with no danger.

00:12:15.398 --> 00:12:16.899
With no fear.

00:12:17.358 --> 00:12:20.528
I mean, empowering people to that point

00:12:20.569 --> 00:12:21.696
makes me more humble.

00:12:21.737 --> 00:12:23.906
I don't even know, sometimes I'm like,

00:12:23.948 --> 00:12:26.701
"Where is the hole that I can drive in
and then disappear,

00:12:26.701 --> 00:12:28.160
and let them be."

00:12:28.536 --> 00:12:29.829
But they won't let me go.

00:12:29.829 --> 00:12:31.872
They say, "We want you
to come and see us,

00:12:31.914 --> 00:12:33.291
and then we learn from you."

00:12:33.332 --> 00:12:37.420
But I learn more from those girls
than I can ever learn from anybody.

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:41.132
The joy of being onstage is different,

00:12:41.132 --> 00:12:44.552
it's planting seeds worldwide

00:12:44.552 --> 00:12:47.513
for people to see their capacity,

00:12:47.555 --> 00:12:49.056
what they are capable of.

00:12:49.098 --> 00:12:52.143
How many times after concert
people will come to me and say,

00:12:52.184 --> 00:12:54.228
"Angelique, how can we help?"

00:12:54.729 --> 00:12:56.605
And I'm like, "How can you help?

00:12:56.605 --> 00:12:58.232
Come on.

00:12:58.232 --> 00:12:59.442
Your neighbors.

00:12:59.442 --> 00:13:01.235
Start looking at them.

00:13:01.277 --> 00:13:04.155
You can volunteer your time for homework,

00:13:04.196 --> 00:13:06.407
there's so much you can do
in your own country.

00:13:06.449 --> 00:13:10.077
Don't think that Africa is just
the country where we need help.

00:13:10.077 --> 00:13:11.620
We don't need help.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.331
We need collaboration."

00:13:14.373 --> 00:13:16.709
FO: Did you warm up your voice today?

00:13:17.001 --> 00:13:18.294
AK: Yes, I did.

00:13:19.378 --> 00:13:24.925
FO: What do you sing to yourself
to cheer yourself up?

00:13:24.925 --> 00:13:26.427
What do you sing and is like,

00:13:26.427 --> 00:13:29.305
"Oh, that was better than Prozac."

00:13:30.181 --> 00:13:34.226
AK: (Singing)

00:14:01.587 --> 00:14:02.838
(Laughs)

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:04.548
We can sit here for days.

00:14:06.133 --> 00:14:07.927
FO: So glad you warmed up.

00:14:08.761 --> 00:14:14.433
When you see your Grammys lined up,

00:14:14.475 --> 00:14:16.727
and they're all shiny and extraordinary,

00:14:16.727 --> 00:14:19.647
I'm not even going to name how many,
because every time I do,

00:14:19.647 --> 00:14:22.983
you get more and then this chat will date,

00:14:23.025 --> 00:14:27.154
so the many, multiple Grammys
that you have won,

00:14:27.196 --> 00:14:28.823
when you look at them,

00:14:28.864 --> 00:14:30.074
Do you, "Whoo!"

00:14:30.116 --> 00:14:31.659
(Laughs)

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:32.910
Does it feel good?

00:14:32.952 --> 00:14:34.245
AK: It feels good.

00:14:34.286 --> 00:14:38.833
And at the same time, as I said before,

00:14:38.874 --> 00:14:40.543
it is a responsibility.

00:14:41.627 --> 00:14:44.463
I mean, my work,

00:14:44.463 --> 00:14:48.926
I've been nominated first time
at the Grammys, it was in 1993, '94.

00:14:50.428 --> 00:14:52.930
And the first Grammy I won was 2007.

00:14:54.515 --> 00:14:56.642
And I said to people,

00:14:56.684 --> 00:14:58.644
don't take Grammy for granted.

00:14:58.686 --> 00:15:01.856
It's not about how many likes you have,

00:15:01.897 --> 00:15:03.441
it's what you have to say.

00:15:03.858 --> 00:15:05.818
What you are bringing to the table.

00:15:06.485 --> 00:15:09.029
How you are impacting the world

00:15:09.071 --> 00:15:12.575
and how you are changing
the music business as it is.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.911
And for me,

00:15:15.911 --> 00:15:19.498
that responsibility is always
at the core of everything I do.

00:15:20.374 --> 00:15:23.711
When I'm setting myself
to write new songs,

00:15:23.711 --> 00:15:25.421
I don't think about Grammys.

00:15:25.463 --> 00:15:27.006
All I think about,

00:15:27.047 --> 00:15:29.884
how this song would help people.

00:15:30.926 --> 00:15:32.887
What impact is it going to have?

00:15:34.388 --> 00:15:36.098
FO: I know you plan.

00:15:36.098 --> 00:15:39.894
I know you have ideas
about what you want to do next.

00:15:39.935 --> 00:15:41.896
For many artists, it would be, you know,

00:15:41.896 --> 00:15:44.273
how do you want to spend
your sunset years?

00:15:44.315 --> 00:15:48.152
But you have extraordinary
longevity in your family.

00:15:48.194 --> 00:15:51.113
So you have another
half a lifetime still to go.

00:15:51.155 --> 00:15:52.323
(Laughter)

00:15:52.364 --> 00:15:56.785
We are going to be going to 100-year-old
Angélique Kidjo concert.

00:15:56.827 --> 00:15:58.829
And you're still going to be, "Let's go."

00:15:58.829 --> 00:16:00.414
AK: Yeah, I'll be running around.

00:16:00.456 --> 00:16:05.294
FO: So it feels kind of weird
to talk about legacy.

00:16:05.294 --> 00:16:09.381
But what do you want
people who follow your work,

00:16:09.423 --> 00:16:13.594
admire your work, see joy in your work,

00:16:13.636 --> 00:16:18.390
what do you want them
to take away from it?

00:16:18.682 --> 00:16:23.938
AK: Well, what I want people
to take away from my work

00:16:23.979 --> 00:16:26.815
is their own worth, people's worth.

00:16:28.025 --> 00:16:31.111
We have too many people that think
that they are worth nothing.

00:16:31.111 --> 00:16:33.614
And that hurts me.

00:16:35.115 --> 00:16:40.746
And when you give birth to a child,

00:16:40.788 --> 00:16:44.792
when a human being is coming
out of your body,

00:16:44.833 --> 00:16:46.502
everything changes.

00:16:46.544 --> 00:16:49.547
Priorities,

00:16:49.588 --> 00:16:50.881
what matters,

00:16:50.881 --> 00:16:53.425
you learn to go to the core of things.

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:58.013
What I want people to remember is that

00:16:58.013 --> 00:17:01.600
you can fall beneath the Earth.

00:17:02.601 --> 00:17:04.645
But you always can rise.

00:17:04.645 --> 00:17:06.939
Does not matter how hard it is.

00:17:06.939 --> 00:17:09.692
Get up, and live your life.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.820
You know it, and that's how we roll.

00:17:14.113 --> 00:17:15.281
FO: I love you.

00:17:15.322 --> 00:17:16.490
AK: Me too.

00:17:16.532 --> 00:17:19.159
Since the first time I met,
it was a love story.

00:17:19.201 --> 00:17:20.661
(Laughter)

